<h2>How the case arose</h2>

<p>The respondent was elected to the Karnataka Legislative Assembly from the Bidar constituency in 2023. The appellant lodged a private complaint dated 21 August 2023 under Section 200 CrPC before the 42nd Additional Chief Metropolitan Magistrate, Bengaluru (PCR No. 9254 of 2023, later CC No. 29813 of 2023), alleging that the respondent had given a false residential address in his nomination affidavit — an offence under Section 125A. The respondent moved the Karnataka High Court in W.P. No. 7458 of 2024, and by judgment of 7 March 2025 the High Court quashed the complaint, holding that the offence attracted Section 195(1) CrPC, so that only the Election Commission could set the law in motion. The Supreme Court granted leave on 20 July 2026.</p>

<h2>The legal question: who guards the affidavit?</h2>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>High Court's view</h3><p>A false statement in an election affidavit is akin to offences against public justice; Section 195(1) CrPC applies, and a private complaint is barred — the Election Commission alone may complain.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>Supreme Court's holding</h3><p>Section 125A is deliberately omitted from the list in Section 195 CrPC. The rigour of Section 195 does not apply to a private complaint under Section 200 CrPC alleging a Section 125A offence; no court is barred from taking cognizance on such a complaint.</p></div>
</div>

<p>Justice Dipankar Datta, writing for the Bench, grounded the conclusion in the statutory text. Section 195 CrPC is an exhaustive, strictly construed exception to the ordinary rule that anyone can set the criminal law in motion; offences outside its enumeration remain governed by the ordinary rule. Section 125A, with its non-obstante opening, creates a freestanding electoral offence that Parliament chose not to route through any designated complainant. The Court also held that press notes or administrative positions of the Election Commission cannot control the interpretation of the statute. The High Court was, in the Court's words, clearly in error in returning the finding that it did.</p>

<h2>Winning the point, not the case</h2>

<p>The judgment then took a turn familiar to Supreme Court practice under Article 136: the declaration of law was divorced from the fate of the litigation. Examining the record, the Court found the specific allegation — that the disclosed residential address was false — had been more than effectively rebutted by the respondent's material, making the prospect of conviction remote. Reviving a complaint destined to fail would serve no one. The closure of proceedings before the trial Magistrate was therefore upheld, but expressly for reasons different from those assigned by the High Court, and the appeal was disposed of with parties bearing their own costs.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This structure — correcting the High Court's law while sustaining its result — illustrates a recurring feature of Article 136 jurisdiction: the Supreme Court sits to settle principle, not merely to referee outcomes. A litigant can lose the appeal yet change the law; the respondent keeps his victory, while every future complainant inherits the corrected rule.</p></div>

<h2>The practical map after Madki</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Affidavit scrutiny</h3><p>A voter examines the Form 26 affidavit filed with the nomination — assets, liabilities, cases, particulars.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Private complaint</h3><p>A complaint under Section 200 CrPC / Section 223 BNSS is filed before the competent Magistrate alleging the Section 125A offence, with supporting material.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Magistrate's filter</h3><p>Cognizance, examination of the complainant, and the Section 202 CrPC / Section 225 BNSS inquiry remain fully applicable — the bar is gone, the burden is not.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Trial on merits</h3><p>Conviction still requires proof of a false statement or concealment in the affidavit; rebutted or trivial allegations will fail, as this very case shows.</p></div>
</div>

<p>Two cautions complete the picture. First, Section 125A punishes falsity in the nomination affidavit; it is distinct from an election petition under Section 100 of the RP Act, which challenges the election itself and runs on its own strict timelines before the High Court. A criminal complaint is not a substitute for an election petition. Second, the judgment does not convert election season into open season: the Magistrate's scrutiny at the cognizance stage remains the filter against vexatious complaints, and this case — where the law point was won but the complaint stayed closed — is itself the demonstration.</p>
